{"qid":"Q151536","names":["Novgorod Republic"],"title":"Novgorod Republic","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Novgorod Republic was a mercantile city-state in northwestern Russia that expelled its Rurikid prince in 1136 and thereafter governed itself through a veche (popular assembly) that elected its posadnik (mayor) and invited or dismissed princes as military commanders. At its height in the 13th–14th centuries Novgorod's commercial empire stretched from the Baltic to the Ural Mountains and the White Sea, making it a key node of the Hanseatic trade network and a supplier of furs, wax, and amber to western Europe. Ivan III of Moscow besieged the city, defeated the Novgorodian forces at Shelon in 1471, and completed annexation in 1478 by abolishing the veche and deporting the leading merchant families to central Russia, ending the republic's nearly three and a half centuries of self-governance.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Novgorod's core city territory and immediate farmland are well-documented through birch-bark documents (berestyanye gramoty) and chronicle sources; the vast northern and eastern fur-gathering dependencies — extending to Perm and the Urals — are loosely attested through tribute records and are mapped with moderate uncertainty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Veliky Novgorod","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Veliky-Novgorod"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Novgorod Republic","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novgorod_Republic"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Novgorod Republic","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Mstislav the Great","nativeName":"Мстислав Владим